Z||000019||EdsB52||08-09-2001||05:12 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR>[B]ok lets get this straight once and for all.<P>I think the legislature just repeals laws in this case, as opposed to passing laws that conflict and supersede former laws. I'm not an expert in the area of legislation, so that issue is up for grabs.<P>"so apparently they use to have laws that prevented you from doing stuff that was a crime against nature"<P>Actually most states probably still do. Many laws are left on the books, even though they are unenforceable, on purpose in the case of an appellate court overturning previous precedent, the State isn't burdened into passing that law again. Remember, statute is a bottom-feeder when it comes to laws, yet police and prosecutors pass it off as gospel. Here's an example; most States have as statute laws prohibiting abortion, Roe vs. Wade supercedes all State laws, State Appellate decisions, and US Circuit Court decisions through the 14th Amendment that makes the US Constitution apply to all States. Yet these States leave the laws on the books. I don't find it unscrupulous that States do this, but the reason is obvious. If W. Baloney and his contingency remain in office long enough, Roe will be overturned (along with Miranda, Terry, and other key Stare Decisis) and State law may/will again preside. I actually haven't been a fan of female politicians most of my life and have often said we would have a black man as President before we have a female. I think I will recant. I like our Governor.<P>"but now those laws are illegal."<P>Those laws were probably unenforceable anyway. I think it’s been 50 years since the last prosecution for open and notorious cohabitation. Why don't they repeal some laws that need to be repealed?<P>"so if wearing clothes was a crime against nature, then they could of had law against doing that, wearing clothes."<P>Generally, the Constitution gives us rights, protections, and guarantees while laws restrict or repeal those rights. This is the key point as to why I switched from card-carrying Republican to Democrat. If you look at conservative legislatures vs. liberal ones, you'll find much more restriction being passed by the former. Most importantly you'll find US Supreme Court decisions benefit the government and the rich. If anyone would like to argue this (US Supreme Court), I would more than welcome it. Just recite some decisions handed down by the US Supreme Court that still stand that benefit the average citizen, or all citizens, that were decided by conservative courts. There are some out there. This is why I back the Dems. Once you stop waving the flag and look at the substantive elements, most common men will see the light and opt for the Dems.||||reg||1
