Z||000028||EdsB52||08-11-2001||09:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>What's the big deal about the topless thing? It's not usually a case of "I want to but I can't." It's a case of "Should I want to, I should be able to, because men have nipples just like mine, only tiny." We had a parade of sorts after that law passed. A bunch of feminist-types were going to march topless from legislature building. 4 women showed up, with 500 men. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree in part. I think the fundamental driver behind women protesting to go topless is based on equality. To state, "should I want to" or "should I be able to" is a little more passive than the women driving the issue would feel. I think when women finally prevail with this issue it’ll fall under the category of, "careful what you wish for, you might just get it." Because when these women grow older there might be a generation of young girls exercising the rights their mothers won for them, hence creating a generation of women that buy into objectification of women. It's kind of a double-edge sword for women's rights activists. But I think NOW, formerly run by Patricia Ireland wants whatever rights men have and that's not an unfair request. But I think women's rights activists forget that the goose/gander expression is more a figure of speech than a literal rule. What's good for the goose might be disaster for the gander and vice versa.<P>One irony I've noticed is that women follow men’s trends in an attempt to establish their independence. Women have followed trends such as smoking, driving racecars, boxing, and hundreds of other male dominated sports, activities, and occupations that are traditionally male dominated. On one hand they must follow men to break sexist stereotypes, but on the other they show further admiration to men when they engage in our activities. Although, I don't blame them for wanting to break out of the old-school subjugation that has been present for hundreds of years. The one particularly perplexing attribute of liberating women everywhere centers on sexuality. Guys love a women’s ass for obvious reasons; its functionality. If a woman has a nice, small, tight ass, we as pigs (men) find that woman to be of more value. We have established our sexuality value system around that body part and we did it first. I don't know when women started finding ideal the ass of a man, but if you watch movies as a reflection of society I would say around the late seventies/early eighties. A mans ass has no function to a female, but it happens to be the standard to which we adjudge women, so now all of the sudden we will be held to the same standard. I can't find another reason to rationalize women's affinity to a man's ass, probably arising out of the liberation era.<P>But ultimately I agree with women in their plight to break the age-old male stereotype roles. As a somewhat distant example, when I was in the A.F., I didn't like some prick telling me I had to wear my hair short for no other practical purpose than to establish dominance over me. As well they disallowed us to frequent a head shop. When I got out, I wore my hair military short for several years to follow and have never visited a head shop, so it wasn't me wanting to engage in certain conduct/appearance, it was me not wanting to be ordered to conform to someone else’s standard of ideal, or in this case moral.||||reg||1
